    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter June 11 with a clear centerpiece in **Pete Crow-Armstrong**, who earns a dominant 82.13 score against Feltner. This matchup represents our ideal scenario: **Pete Crow-Armstrong** benefits from a massive 14% park factor boost and a 13% SP skill advantage, while his leadoff spot provides the high-volume plate appearances we crave for stolen base accumulation. While **Chase Meidroth** (61.90) is also a strong play atop the lineup against Mart\u00edn P\u00e9rez, it is **Pete Crow-Armstrong** who possesses the highest ceiling today given the favorable xERA of his opposing starter and his elite speed profile, which remains our primary objective in this 5x5 Roto landscape.\n\nWe are navigating several \"Lineup Pending\" situations, most notably for **Chase Meidroth**, **Jacob Gonzalez**, and **Luke Raley**. While **Chase Meidroth** is projected for a strong day, **Jacob Gonzalez** sits on the cusp of our marginal threshold at 40.65; his platoon disadvantage against a lefty in Mart\u00edn P\u00e9rez suppresses his output, making him a candidate to be benched if a superior defensive or high-contact bat becomes available off the wire. Meanwhile, **Luke Raley** (54.19) provides a significant park-adjusted boost despite facing a tougher arm in Kyle Bradish. His utility floor is buoyed by a 16% park factor, though we are monitoring his status closely, as any shift lower in the batting order would jeopardize his daily efficiency score.\n\nThe most difficult bench decision involves balancing **Dillon Dingler** (44.03) against the internal depth of our roster. **Dillon Dingler** is currently starting due to position scarcity, though his dynamic platoon penalty against right-handed pitching drags his score downward. We opted to keep him in the lineup over bench options like **Josh Smith** or **Casey Schmitt** because his presence in the #4 spot offers a superior RBI opportunity compared to the current alternatives. We are keeping a close watch on **Colt Keith** (58.22), whose 7% platoon advantage against Matthews makes him a reliable anchor at third base, provided he can capitalize on the slow delivery of the opposing starter to manufacture an opportunistic stolen base.",
